#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:1
msgid "Safer Bash Shebang Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:3
msgid ""
"If you’re writing a `bash` shebang recipe, consider adding `set -euxo "
"pipefail`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:5
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env bash\n"
"  set -euxo pipefail\n"
"  hello='Yo'\n"
"  echo \"$hello from Bash!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:13
msgid ""
"It isn’t strictly necessary, but `set -euxo pipefail` turns on a few useful "
"features that make `bash` shebang recipes behave more like normal, shell "
"`just` recipes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:17
msgid "`set -e` makes `bash` exit if a command fails."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:19
msgid "`set -u` makes `bash` exit if a variable is undefined."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:21
msgid "`set -x` makes `bash` print each script line before it’s run."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:23
msgid ""
"`set -o pipefail` makes `bash` exit if a command in a pipeline fails. This "
"is `bash`\\-specific, so isn’t turned on in normal shell `just` recipes."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:26
msgid "Together, these avoid a lot of shell scripting gotchas."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:28
msgid "Shebang Recipe Execution on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:30
msgid ""
"On Windows, shebang interpreter paths containing a `/` are translated from "
"Unix-style paths to Windows-style paths using `cygpath`, a utility that "
"ships with [Cygwin](http://www.cygwin.com)."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:34
msgid "For example, to execute this recipe on Windows:"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:36
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"echo:\n"
"  #!/bin/sh\n"
"  echo \"Hello!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:42
msgid ""
"The interpreter path `/bin/sh` will be translated to a Windows-style path "
"using `cygpath` before being executed."
msgstr ""

#: src/safer-bash-shebang-recipes.md:45
msgid ""
"If the interpreter path does not contain a `/` it will be executed without "
"being translated. This is useful if `cygpath` is not available, or you wish "
"to pass a Windows-style path to the interpreter."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:1
msgid "Script and Shebang Recipe Temporary Files"
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:3
msgid ""
"Both script and shebang recipes write the recipe body to a temporary file "
"for execution. Script recipes execute that file by passing it to a command, "
"while shebang recipes execute the file directly. Shebang recipe execution "
"will fail if the filesystem containing the temporary file is mounted with "
"`noexec` or is otherwise non-executable."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:9
msgid ""
"The directory that `just` writes temporary files to may be configured in a "
"number of ways, from highest to lowest precedence:"
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:12
msgid ""
"Globally with the `--tempdir` command-line option or the `JUST_TEMPDIR` "
"environment variable<sup>1.41.0</sup>."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:15
msgid "On a per-module basis with the `tempdir` setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:17
msgid "Globally on Linux with the `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` environment variable."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-and-shebang-recipe-temporary-files.md:19
msgid ""
"Falling back to the directory returned by [std::env::temp_dir](https://"
"doc.rust-lang.org/std/env/fn.temp_dir.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-recipes.md:1
msgid "Script Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: src/script-recipes.md:3
msgid ""
"Recipes with a `[script(COMMAND)]`<sup>1.32.0</sup> attribute are run as "
"scripts interpreted by `COMMAND`. This avoids some of the issues with "
"shebang recipes, such as the use of `cygpath` on Windows, the need to use `/"
"usr/bin/env`, inconsistencies in shebang line splitting across Unix OSs, and "
"requiring a temporary directory from which files can be executed."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-recipes.md:9
msgid ""
"Recipes with an empty `[script]` attribute are executed with the value of "
"`set script-interpreter := […]`<sup>1.33.0</sup>, defaulting to `sh -eu`, "
"and _not_ the value of `set shell`."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-recipes.md:12
msgid ""
"The body of the recipe is evaluated, written to disk in the temporary "
"directory, and run by passing its path as an argument to `COMMAND`."
msgstr ""

#: src/script-recipes.md:15
msgid ""
"With `set default-script := true`<sup>1.52.0</sup>, recipes default to "
"script recipes instead of shell recipes, unless overridden with the "
"`[shell]` attribute<sup>1.52.0</sup>."
msgstr ""

#: src/setting-variables-in-a-recipe.md:1
msgid "Setting Variables in a Recipe"
msgstr ""

#: src/setting-variables-in-a-recipe.md:3
msgid ""
"Recipe lines are interpreted by the shell, not `just`, so it’s not possible "
"to set `just` variables in the middle of a recipe:"
msgstr ""

#: src/setting-variables-in-a-recipe.md:6
msgid ""
"```justfile\n"
"foo:\n"
"  x := \"hello\" # This doesn't work!\n"
"  echo {{x}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/setting-variables-in-a-recipe.md:12
msgid ""
"It is possible to use shell variables, but there’s another problem. Every "
"recipe line is run by a new shell instance, so variables set in one line "
"won’t be set in the next:"
msgstr ""

#: src/setting-variables-in-a-recipe.md:23
msgid ""
"The best way to work around this is to use a shebang recipe. Shebang recipe "
"bodies are extracted and run as scripts, so a single shell instance will run "
"the whole thing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:1
msgid "Shebang Recipes"
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:3
msgid ""
"Recipes that start with `#!` are called shebang recipes, and are executed by "
"saving the recipe body to a file and running it. This lets you write recipes "
"in different languages:"
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:7
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"polyglot: python js perl sh ruby nu\n"
"\n"
"python:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env python3\n"
"  print('Hello from python!')\n"
"\n"
"js:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env node\n"
"  console.log('Greetings from JavaScript!')\n"
"\n"
"perl:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env perl\n"
"  print \"Larry Wall says Hi!\\n\";\n"
"\n"
"sh:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env sh\n"
"  hello='Yo'\n"
"  echo \"$hello from a shell script!\"\n"
"\n"
"nu:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env nu\n"
"  let hello = 'Hola'\n"
"  echo $\"($hello) from a nushell script!\"\n"
"\n"
"ruby:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env ruby\n"
"  puts \"Hello from ruby!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:47
msgid ""
"On Unix-like operating systems, including Linux and macOS, shebang recipes "
"are executed by saving the recipe body to a file in a temporary directory, "
"marking the file as executable, and executing it. The OS then parses the "
"shebang line into a command line and invokes it, including the path to the "
"file. For example, if a recipe starts with `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, the final "
"command that the OS runs will be something like `/usr/bin/env bash /tmp/"
"PATH_TO_SAVED_RECIPE_BODY`."
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:54
msgid ""
"Shebang line splitting is operating system dependent. When passing a command "
"with arguments, you may need to tell `env` to split them explicitly by using "
"the `-S` flag:"
msgstr ""

#: src/shebang-recipes.md:64
msgid ""
"Windows does not support shebang lines. On Windows, `just` splits the "
"shebang line into a command and arguments, saves the recipe body to a file, "
"and invokes the split command and arguments, adding the path to the saved "
"recipe body as the final argument. For example, on Windows, if a recipe "
"starts with `#! py`, the final command the OS runs will be something like "
"`py C:\\Temp\\PATH_TO_SAVED_RECIPE_BODY`."
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:1
msgid "Shell"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:3
msgid ""
"The `shell` setting controls the command used to invoke recipe lines and "
"backticks. Shebang recipes are unaffected. The default shell is `sh -cu`."
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:6
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"# use python3 to execute recipe lines and backticks\n"
"set shell := [\"python3\", \"-c\"]\n"
"\n"
"# use print to capture result of evaluation\n"
"foos := `print(\"foo\" * 4)`\n"
"\n"
"foo:\n"
"  print(\"Snake snake snake snake.\")\n"
"  print(\"{{foos}}\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:18
msgid ""
"`just` passes the command to be executed as an argument. Many shells will "
"need an additional flag, often `-c`, to make them evaluate the first "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:21
msgid "Windows Shell"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:23
msgid ""
"`just` uses `sh` on Windows by default. To use a different shell on Windows, "
"use the `[windows]` attribute on the `shell` setting:"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:26
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"[windows]\n"
"set shell := [\"powershell.exe\", \"-NoLogo\", \"-Command\"]\n"
"\n"
"hello:\n"
"  Write-Host \"Hello, world!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:34
msgid ""
"See [powershell.just](https://github.com/casey/just/blob/master/examples/"
"powershell.just) for a justfile that uses PowerShell on all platforms."
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:38
msgid "Python 3"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:40
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set shell := [\"python3\", \"-c\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:46
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set shell := [\"bash\", \"-uc\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:50
msgid "Z Shell"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:52
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set shell := [\"zsh\", \"-uc\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:58
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set shell := [\"fish\", \"-c\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:64
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set shell := [\"nu\", \"-c\"]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:68
msgid "If you want to change the default table mode to `light`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/shell.md:74
msgid ""
"_[Nushell](https://github.com/nushell/nushell) was written in Rust, and "
"**has cross-platform support for Windows / macOS and Linux**._"
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:1
msgid "Sigils"
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:3
msgid ""
"Commands in shell recipes may be prefixed with any combination of the sigils "
"`-`, `@`, and `?`."
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:6
msgid "The `@` sigil toggles command echoing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:8
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo:\n"
"  @echo \"This line won't be echoed!\"\n"
"  echo \"This line will be echoed!\"\n"
"\n"
"@bar:\n"
"  @echo \"This line will be echoed!\"\n"
"  echo \"This line won't be echoed!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:18
msgid ""
"The `-` sigil causes recipe execution to continue even if the command "
"returns a nonzero exit status:"
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:29
msgid ""
"The `?` sigil<sup>1.47.0</sup> causes the current recipe to stop executing "
"if the command exits with status code `1`, however execution of other "
"recipes will continue. Exit status `0` causes the current recipe to continue "
"execution as normal. All other exit codes are reserved and should not be "
"used, as they may be given meaning in a future version of `just`."
msgstr ""

#: src/sigils.md:35
msgid ""
"If the `guards` setting is unset or false, `?` sigils are ignored and "
"instead treated as part of the command."
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions.md:1
msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: src/allow-duplicate-variables.md:1
msgid "Allow Duplicate Variables"
msgstr ""

#: src/allow-duplicate-variables.md:3
msgid ""
"If `allow-duplicate-variables` is set to `true`, defining multiple variables "
"with the same name is not an error and the last definition is used. Defaults "
"to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: src/allow-duplicate-variables.md:7
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set allow-duplicate-variables\n"
"\n"
"a := \"foo\"\n"
"a := \"bar\"\n"
"\n"
"@foo:\n"
"  echo {{a}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/built-in-functions.md:1
msgid "Built-in Functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/built-in-functions.md:3
msgid ""
"`just` provides many built-in functions for use in expressions, including "
"recipe body `{{…}}` substitutions, assignments, and default parameter values."
msgstr ""

#: src/built-in-functions.md:6
msgid ""
"All functions ending in `_directory` can be abbreviated to `_dir`. So "
"`home_directory()` can also be written as `home_dir()`. In addition, "
"`invocation_directory_native()` can be abbreviated to "
"`invocation_dir_native()`."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:1
msgid "Command Evaluation Using Backticks"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:3
msgid "Backticks can be used to store the result of commands:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:5
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"localhost := `dumpinterfaces | cut -d: -f2 | sed 's/\\/.*//' | sed 's/ //"
"g'`\n"
"\n"
"serve:\n"
"  ./serve {{localhost}} 8080\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:12
msgid ""
"Indented backticks, delimited by three backticks, are de-indented in the "
"same manner as indented strings:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:23
msgid "See the [Strings](strings.html) section for details on unindenting."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:25
msgid ""
"Backticks may not start with `#!`. This syntax is reserved for a future "
"upgrade."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-evaluation-using-backticks.md:28
msgid ""
"The [`shell(…)` function](functions.html#external-commands) provides a more "
"general mechanism to invoke external commands, including the ability to "
"execute the contents of a variable as a command, and to pass arguments to a "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:1
msgid "Conditional Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:3
msgid ""
"`if`/`else` expressions evaluate different branches depending on if two "
"expressions evaluate to the same value:"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:6
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if \"2\" == \"2\" { \"Good!\" } else { \"1984\" }\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  @echo \"{{foo}}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:18
msgid "It is also possible to test for inequality:"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:20
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if \"hello\" != \"goodbye\" { \"xyz\" } else { \"abc\" }\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  @echo {{foo}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:32
msgid "And match against regular expressions:"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:34
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if \"hello\" =~ 'hel+o' { \"match\" } else { \"mismatch\" }\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  @echo {{foo}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:46
msgid ""
"Regular expressions are provided by the [regex crate](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/regex), whose syntax is documented on [docs.rs](https://docs.rs/"
"regex/1.5.4/regex/#syntax). Since regular expressions commonly use backslash "
"escape sequences, consider using single-quoted string literals, which will "
"pass slashes to the regex parser unmolested."
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:52
msgid ""
"Conditional expressions short-circuit, which means they only evaluate one of "
"their branches. This can be used to make sure that backtick expressions "
"don’t run when they shouldn’t."
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:56
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if env_var(\"RELEASE\") == \"true\" { `get-something-from-release-"
"database` } else { \"dummy-value\" }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:60
msgid "Conditionals can be used inside of recipes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:62
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"bar foo:\n"
"  echo {{ if foo == \"bar\" { \"hello\" } else { \"goodbye\" } }}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:67
msgid "Multiple conditionals can be chained:"
msgstr ""

#: src/conditional-expressions.md:69
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if \"hello\" == \"goodbye\" {\n"
"  \"xyz\"\n"
"} else if \"a\" == \"a\" {\n"
"  \"abc\"\n"
"} else {\n"
"  \"123\"\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  @echo {{foo}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:1
msgid "Expressions and Substitutions"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:3
msgid ""
"Various operators and function calls are supported in expressions, which may "
"be used in assignments, default recipe arguments, and inside recipe body "
"`{{…}}` substitutions."
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:7
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"tmpdir  := `mktemp -d`\n"
"version := \"0.2.7\"\n"
"tardir  := tmpdir / \"awesomesauce-\" + version\n"
"tarball := tardir + \".tar.gz\"\n"
"config  := quote(config_dir() / \".project-config\")\n"
"\n"
"publish:\n"
"  rm -f {{tarball}}\n"
"  mkdir {{tardir}}\n"
"  cp README.md *.c {{ config }} {{tardir}}\n"
"  tar zcvf {{tarball}} {{tardir}}\n"
"  scp {{tarball}} me@server.com:release/\n"
"  rm -rf {{tarball}} {{tardir}}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:23
msgid "Concatenation"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:25
msgid ""
"The `+` operator returns the left-hand argument concatenated with the right-"
"hand argument:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:32
msgid "Logical Operators"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:34
msgid ""
"The logical operators `&&` and `||` can be used to coalesce "
"values<sup>1.37.0</sup>, similar to Python’s `and` and `or`. The only false "
"value is the empty list `[]`; every other value, including the empty string "
"`''`, is true."
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:39
msgid ""
"These operators require `set lists`<sup>1.53.0</sup>, which is currently "
"unstable."
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:42
msgid ""
"The `&&` operator returns the empty list if the left-hand argument is false, "
"otherwise it returns the right-hand argument:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:50
msgid ""
"The `||` operator returns the left-hand argument if it is true, otherwise it "
"returns the right-hand argument:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:58
msgid "Joining Paths"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:60
msgid "The `/` operator can be used to join two strings with a slash:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:62
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := \"a\" / \"b\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:71
msgid "Note that a `/` is added even if one is already present:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:73
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := \"a/\"\n"
"bar := foo / \"b\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:78
msgid ""
"```\n"
"$ just --evaluate bar\n"
"a//b\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:83
msgid "Absolute paths can also be constructed<sup>1.5.0</sup>:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:85
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := / \"b\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:94
msgid ""
"The `/` operator uses the `/` character, even on Windows. Thus, using the `/"
"` operator should be avoided with paths that use universal naming convention "
"(UNC), i.e., those that start with `\\\\`, since forward slashes are not "
"supported with UNC paths."
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:99
msgid "Escaping `{{`"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:101
msgid "To write a recipe containing `{{`, use `{{{{`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:108
msgid "(An unmatched `}}` is ignored, so it doesn’t need to be escaped.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:110
msgid ""
"Another option is to put all the text you’d like to escape inside of an "
"interpolation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:118
msgid "Yet another option is to use `{{ \"{{\" }}`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/expressions-and-substitutions.md:120
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"braces:\n"
"  echo 'I {{ \"{{\" }}LOVE}} curly braces!'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/lazy.md:1
msgid "Lazy"
msgstr ""

#: src/lazy.md:3
msgid ""
"The `lazy` setting<sup>1.47.0</sup> causes the evaluator to skip evaluating "
"unused variables. This can be beneficial when a `justfile` contains "
"variables that are expensive to evaluate but only sometimes used."
msgstr ""

#: src/lazy.md:7
msgid ""
"In the following `justfile`, `token` will be skipped when only invoking "
"`bar`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/lazy.md:9
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set lazy\n"
"\n"
"token := `expensive-script-to-get-credentials`\n"
"\n"
"foo:\n"
"  curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer {{ token }}\" https://example.com/foo\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  cargo test\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/lazy.md:21
msgid ""
"Because `just` cannot determine when exported variables are used, "
"assignments with `export` and assignments in a module with `set export` will "
"always be evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:1
msgid "Lists"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:3
msgid ""
"The `lists` setting<sup>1.53.0</sup> allows values that are lists of "
"strings. It is currently unstable and will change in backwards incompatible "
"ways. This section documents changes in behavior when `set lists` is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:7
msgid ""
"It has not yet been decided how lists should behave with many of the built-"
"in functions. Functions that have been updated to accept lists are mentioned "
"in this section. Using lists with any other function is an error. The "
"`join_list()` function can be used to convert lists into space-separated "
"strings for use with un-upgraded functions. Feedback on how built-in "
"functions should behave with lists, and on lists in general, is most "
"welcome! Feel free to open an issue or leave a comment in the [`set lists` "
"tracking issue](https://github.com/casey/just/issues/3377)."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:16
msgid ""
"Variadic recipe parameters are lists of strings instead of single space-"
"separated strings."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:19
msgid ""
"List literals are written `[a, b, c]`. List literals flatten their "
"arguments, since lists may only contain strings and not other lists. For "
"example, `[[\"a\", \"b\"], [], \"c\"]` evaluates to `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:23
msgid ""
"Lists in recipe and `f`\\-string interpolations are joined with spaces into "
"a single string."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:26
msgid ""
"Each argument to a dependency binds to exactly one parameter, and supplying "
"extra arguments to a variadic dependency is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:29
msgid ""
"Dependencies may be invoked once per element of a list with `*(recipe "
"*argument)`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:32
msgid ""
"A parameter evaluates to the default when the argument is the empty list."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:34
msgid ""
"Passing an empty list to a non-`*` parameter without a default is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:36
msgid ""
"The `else` of an `if` may be omitted, in which case the `if` evaluates to "
"`[]` when its condition is false."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:39
msgid ""
"Message values in `assert(condition, message)` and `[confirm(message)]` are "
"space-joined for display."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:42
msgid ""
"The `+` and `/` operators combine strings and lists. A string and a non-"
"empty list are combined by concatenating the string with each element of the "
"list. Two lists of the same length are combined into a list containing the "
"pairwise concatenated elements of both operands. Combining two lists of "
"different lengths is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:48
msgid "The `++` operator performs list concatenation."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:50
msgid "Booleans"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:52
msgid ""
"The canonical boolean true value is the string `\"true\"`, and the canonical "
"boolean false value is the empty list `[]`. All values other than the empty "
"list are truthy, including `''`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:56
msgid ""
"The condition of an `if` or `assert()` may be any expression, which is "
"evaluated for truthiness."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:59
msgid ""
"The comparison operators `==`, `!=`, `=~`, and `!~` may be used anywhere, "
"not just in `if` and `assert()`, and evaluate to `\"true\"` or `[]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:62
msgid ""
"`value =~ regexes` is true if any element in `value` matches any regex in "
"`regexes`. It is false if either `value` or `regexes` is empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:65
msgid ""
"`value !~ regexes` is true if no element in `value` matches any regex in "
"`regexes`. It is true if either `value` or `regexes` is empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:68
msgid ""
"Values may be negated with `!`. `!expression` evaluates to `\"true\"` if "
"`expression` is `[]`, otherwise it evaluates to `[]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:71
msgid "Settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:73
msgid ""
"The `script-interpreter`, `shell`, and `windows-shell` settings flatten "
"their elements like list literals."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:76
msgid ""
"When `positional-arguments` is set, list arguments are space-joined unless "
"they are variadic, in which case they are passed as one positional argument "
"per element."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:80
msgid ""
"The `--dotenv-filename` and `--dotenv-path` options may be passed multiple "
"times, and the `dotenv-filename` and `dotenv-path` settings accept lists, in "
"which case multiple environment files may be loaded. The values of `dotenv-"
"path` are tried first. If none are found the current directory is searched "
"for the names in `dotenv-filename`, followed by its ancestors, stopping in "
"the first directory that contains any of them and loading all matching files "
"in that directory. If multiple environment files are loaded, variables in "
"files later in list take precedence over earlier ones."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:89
msgid ""
"Each element of the value of `set dotenv-command` is run as a command, with "
"variables from commands later in the list taking precedence over variables "
"from commands earlier in the list."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:93
msgid "Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:95
msgid ""
"The `[arg(flag)]` attribute makes the parameter a flag which does not take a "
"value on the command line. For example, with `[arg('foo', long, flag)]`, "
"`foo` will be `\"true\"` when `--foo` is passed, and `[]` otherwise. Flag "
"parameters may not have a default."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:100
msgid ""
"The `[arg(multiple)]` attribute allows an option or flag to be passed more "
"than once, assigning the list of passed values to the parameter. When "
"combined with `flag` or `value=VALUE`, `\"true\"` or `VALUE`, respectively, "
"are repeated for each occurance of the flag."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:105
msgid ""
"The `[arg(min=MIN)]` and `[arg(max=MAX)]` attributes<sup>1.56.0</sup> can be "
"used to limit the number of values an option or flag may receive."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:108
msgid ""
"The value of `[arg(help)]` may be a list, in which case the help string is "
"the elements of the list joined with spaces. If the list is empty, the "
"argument has no help string."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:112
msgid ""
"The value of `[arg(pattern)]` may be a list, in which case the argument is "
"accepted if it matches any pattern in the list. If the value is the empty "
"list, any argument is accepted. For example, with `[arg('foo', pattern=['--"
"help', '--version'])]`, `foo` may be `--help` or `--version`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:118
msgid ""
"In `[env(variable, value)]` if `value` is `[]`, `variable` is not set. "
"Otherwise it is set to `value` joined with spaces."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:121
msgid "Functions"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:123
msgid "`absolute_path()` - Applies to each list element individually."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:124
msgid ""
"`append()` - Applies to each list element individually and does not split "
"elements on whitespace."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:126
msgid "`assert(condition, message)` - Evaluates to `condition`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:127
msgid ""
"`bool(value)` Converts `value` to the canonical boolean values. Returns `[]` "
"when `value` is `\"\"` `\"0\"` `\"false\"`, or `[]`, and `\"true\"` when "
"`value` is `\"1\"` or `\"true\"`. All other values are an error. Can be used "
"to parse booleans passed as arguments or environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:131
msgid ""
"`env(keys, default)` Checks for the environment variables named in `keys` in "
"order and returns the value of the first that is set. Returns `default` if "
"none are set or an error if `default` is omitted."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:134
msgid "`is_dependency()` - Returns the canonical booleans."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:135
msgid ""
"`join_list(value, separator)` - Joins `value` into a single string. Elements "
"are joined with `separator`, or with a single space if `separator` is "
"omitted."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:138
msgid "`len(value)` - Returns the number of elements in `value`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:139
msgid "`path_exists()` - Returns the canonical booleans."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:140
msgid ""
"`prepend()` - Applies to each list element individually and does not split "
"elements on whitespace."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:142
msgid "`quote()` - Applies to each list element individually."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:143
msgid "`semver_matches()` - Returns the canonical booleans."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:144
msgid ""
"`show(value)` - Converts `value` into a string containing its literal "
"representation. Brackets are used for empty and multi-element lists, e.g., "
"`\"[]\"` and `\"[\"foo\", \"bar\"]\"`, but not single-element lists, e.g., "
"`\"foo\"`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:147
msgid ""
"`split(string, separator)` - Splits `string` into a list on each occurrence "
"of `separator`. If `separator` is omitted, `string` is split on whitespace, "
"with leading and trailing whitespace trimmed."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:150
msgid "`which()` - Returns the empty list when no executable is found."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:154
msgid "Each list element is `quote()`’ed separately:"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:170
msgid ""
"The return value of `quote(args)` is `'bar' 'baz bob'`, instead of `'bar baz "
"bob'`, as would be the case without `set lists`."
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:173
msgid "Variadic positional arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:199
msgid ""
"A mapped dependency is invoked once per element of its starred argument, "
"with `[parallel]` to run them in parallel:"
msgstr ""

#: src/lists.md:219
msgid "The canonical false value `[]` is recommended as a default for options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/stopping-execution-with-error.md:1
msgid "Stopping execution with error"
msgstr ""

#: src/stopping-execution-with-error.md:3
msgid "Execution can be halted with the `error` function. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/stopping-execution-with-error.md:5
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"foo := if \"hello\" == \"goodbye\" {\n"
"  \"xyz\"\n"
"} else if \"a\" == \"b\" {\n"
"  \"abc\"\n"
"} else {\n"
"  error(\"123\")\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/stopping-execution-with-error.md:15
msgid "Which produces the following error when run:"
msgstr ""

#: src/stopping-execution-with-error.md:17
msgid ""
"```\n"
"error: Call to function `error` failed: 123\n"
"   |\n"
"16 |   error(\"123\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:1
msgid "Strings"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:3
msgid ""
"`'single'`, `\"double\"`, and `'''triple'''` quoted string literals are "
"supported. Unlike in recipe bodies, `{{…}}` interpolations are not supported "
"inside strings."
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:7
msgid "Double-quoted strings support escape sequences:"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:9
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"carriage-return   := \"\\r\"\n"
"double-quote      := \"\\\"\"\n"
"newline           := \"\\n\"\n"
"no-newline        := \"\\\n"
"\"\n"
"slash             := \"\\\\\"\n"
"tab               := \"\\t\"\n"
"unicode-codepoint := \"\\u{1F916}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:20
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ just --evaluate\n"
"\"arriage-return   := \"\n"
"double-quote      := \"\"\"\n"
"newline           := \"\n"
"\"\n"
"no-newline        := \"\"\n"
"slash             := \"\\\"\n"
"tab               := \"     \"\n"
"unicode-codepoint := \"🤖\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:32
msgid ""
"The unicode character escape sequence `\\u{…}`<sup>1.36.0</sup> accepts up "
"to six hex digits."
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:35
msgid "Strings may contain line breaks:"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:37
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"single := '\n"
"hello\n"
"'\n"
"\n"
"double := \"\n"
"goodbye\n"
"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:47
msgid "Single-quoted strings do not recognize escape sequences:"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:49
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"escapes := '\\t\\n\\r\\\"\\\\'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:53
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ just --evaluate\n"
"escapes := \"\\t\\n\\r\\\"\\\\\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:58
msgid ""
"Indented versions of both single- and double-quoted strings, delimited by "
"triple single- or double-quotes, are supported. Indented string lines are "
"stripped of a leading line break, and leading whitespace common to all non-"
"blank lines:"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:63
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"# this string will evaluate to `foo\\nbar\\n`\n"
"x := '''\n"
"  foo\n"
"  bar\n"
"'''\n"
"\n"
"# this string will evaluate to `abc\\n  wuv\\nxyz\\n`\n"
"y := \"\"\"\n"
"  abc\n"
"    wuv\n"
"  xyz\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/strings.md:78
msgid ""
"Similar to unindented strings, indented double-quoted strings process escape "
"sequences, and indented single-quoted strings ignore escape sequences. "
"Escape sequence processing takes place after unindentation. The "
"unindentation algorithm does not take escape-sequence produced whitespace or "
"newlines into account."
msgstr ""

