#: src/paths-on-windows.md:1
msgid "Paths on Windows"
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:3
msgid ""
"On Windows, all functions that return paths, except "
"`invocation_directory()`, will return `\\`\\-separated paths. When not using "
"PowerShell or `cmd.exe`, these paths should be quoted to prevent the `\\`s "
"from being interpreted as character escapes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:8
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"ls:\n"
"    echo '{{absolute_path(\".\")}}'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:13
msgid ""
"`cygpath.exe` is an executable included in some distributions of Unix "
"userlands for Windows, including [Cygwin](https://www.cygwin.com/) and [Git]"
"(https://git-scm.com/downloads) for Windows."
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:17
msgid "`just` uses `cygpath.exe` in two places:"
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:19
msgid ""
"For backwards compatibility, `invocation_directory()`, uses `cygpath.exe` to "
"convert the invocation directory into a unix-style `/`\\-separated path. Use "
"`invocation_directory_native()` to get the native, Windows-style path. On "
"unix, `invocation_directory()` and `invocation_directory_native()` both "
"return the same unix-style path."
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:25
msgid ""
"`cygpath.exe` is also used to convert Unix-style shebang lines into Windows "
"paths. As an alternative, the `[script]` attribute can be used, which does "
"not depend on `cygpath.exe`."
msgstr ""

#: src/paths-on-windows.md:29
msgid ""
"If `cygpath.exe` is available, you can use it to convert between path styles:"
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:1
msgid "Printing Complex Strings"
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:3
msgid ""
"`echo` can be used to print strings, but because it processes escape "
"sequences, like `\\n`, and different implementations of `echo` recognize "
"different escape sequences, using `printf` is often a better choice."
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:7
msgid ""
"`printf` takes a C-style format string and any number of arguments, which "
"are interpolated into the format string."
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:10
msgid ""
"This can be combined with indented, triple quoted strings to emulate shell "
"heredocs."
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:13
msgid ""
"Substituting complex strings into recipe bodies with `{{…}}` can also lead "
"to trouble as it may be split by the shell into multiple arguments depending "
"on the presence of whitespace and quotes. Exporting complex strings as "
"environment variables and referring to them with `\"$NAME\"`, note the "
"double quotes, can also help."
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:19
msgid ""
"Putting all this together, to print a string verbatim to standard output, "
"with all its various escape sequences and quotes undisturbed:"
msgstr ""

#: src/printing-complex-strings.md:22
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"export FOO := '''\n"
"  a complicated string with\n"
"  some dis\\tur\\bi\\ng escape sequences\n"
"  and \"quotes\" of 'different' kinds\n"
"'''\n"
"\n"
"bar:\n"
"  printf %s \"$FOO\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:1
msgid "Python Recipes with `uv`"
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:3
msgid ""
"[`uv`](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv) is an excellent cross-platform "
"python project manager, written in Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:6
msgid ""
"Using the `[script]` attribute and `script-interpreter` setting, `just` can "
"easily be configured to run Python recipes with `uv`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:9
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"set script-interpreter := ['uv', 'run', '--script']\n"
"\n"
"[script]\n"
"hello:\n"
"  print(\"Hello from Python!\")\n"
"\n"
"[script]\n"
"goodbye:\n"
"  # /// script\n"
"  # requires-python = \">=3.11\"\n"
"  # dependencies=[\"sh\"]\n"
"  # ///\n"
"  import sh\n"
"  print(sh.echo(\"Goodbye from Python!\"), end='')\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:26
msgid "Of course, a shebang also works:"
msgstr ""

#: src/python-recipes-with-uv.md:28
msgid ""
"```just\n"
"hello:\n"
"  #!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script\n"
"  print(\"Hello from Python!\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

